<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <AssemblyName>NumSharp.Interop.MLNet</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>NumSharp.Interop.MLNet</RootNamespace>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>

    <!-- NuGet package (co-versioned with NumSharp: the version is the repo-root Directory.Build.props
         VersionPrefix, never a number in this file, so the nuspec dependency on NumSharp and this
         package's own version cannot drift apart). -->
    <PackageId>NumSharp.Interop.MLNet</PackageId>
    <!-- Released from 0.80.0-beta1: packable by default (no IsPackable property), packed, signed and published by
         the release workflow's build-nuget job with the other packages, its docs page on the site. -->
    <Authors>Eli Belash, Haiping Chen, Meinrad Recheis</Authors>
    <Product>NumSharp</Product>
    <Company>SciSharp STACK</Company>
    <Copyright>2021 © SciSharp STACK Team</Copyright>
    <Description>Interop between NumSharp NDArray and ML.NET (Microsoft.ML) data. Expose an NDArray to an ML.NET pipeline as an IDataView over the very same unmanaged buffer (no row-by-row POCO fill, no copy — read lazily through the array's strides), read a transformer's output column back as an NDArray, and convert to/from ML.NET's VBuffer vector primitive. Conversion library only: no engine backend, no ModuleInitializer, no native dependency — depends on the standalone Microsoft.ML.DataView contract package (2.0.0 or later) and on NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime for post-processing (Softmax, Argmax, TopK), and composes with whatever Microsoft.ML (MLContext, trainers, transforms) you already reference.</Description>
    <PackageTags>NumSharp;NumPy;MLNet;ML.NET;Microsoft.ML;IDataView;VBuffer;DataView;machine-learning;interop;scientific-computing</PackageTags>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://scisharp.github.io/NumSharp/docs/interop/mlnet.html</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/SciSharp/NumSharp</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE</PackageLicenseFile>
    <!-- No PackageReadmeFile, deliberately: the package embeds no README, so each version's README
         tab stays owner-editable on nuget.org, where the release notes are pasted after publishing.
         README.md in this folder is repo documentation only and is not packed. See "No embedded
         package README" in ../../Directory.Build.props, whose guard fails `dotnet pack` if this returns. -->
    <!-- Packed explicitly (`dotnet pack`), never on build: see the release workflow. License
         acceptance and the icon are repo-wide settings in ../../Directory.Build.props. -->
    <PackageOutputPath>../../packages</PackageOutputPath>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- Symbols: embed the .pdb in the .nupkg (matches NumSharp.Core / the other interop packages). The
         release pipeline uploads/pushes only *.nupkg, so a separate .snupkg would be dropped. -->
    <AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder>$(AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder);.pdb</AllowedOutputExtensionsInPackageBuildOutputFolder>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\LICENSE" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- In-repo: ProjectReference (packs as a NuGet dependency on "NumSharp" $(Version)). -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../NumSharp.Core/NumSharp.Core.csproj" />

    <!-- Post-processing (Softmax, LogSoftmax, Sigmoid, Argmax, TopK) comes from the ONNX Runtime bridge: this package
         references it (packs as a NuGet dependency on "NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime" $(Version)), so an ML.NET user
         calls NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime.Postprocess - the one public Postprocess, where it used to be a second copy
         compiled into this bridge (CS0104 in an app that imported both; the owner's decision of 2026-11-03, F16).
         What it costs an ML.NET-only user: the ONNX Runtime bridge and its Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime.Managed - the C#
         API only (about 250 KB, no native library), resolved at its floor 1.16.0 unless the app references another -
         with System.Memory (and, from ONNX Runtime 1.2x, System.Numerics.Tensors 9). Postprocess touches no ONNX
         Runtime type, so calling it never loads ONNX Runtime. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime/NumSharp.Interop.OnnxRuntime.csproj" />

    <!-- The only ML.NET dependency: the standalone `Microsoft.ML.DataView` package — the contract half of
         ML.NET (IDataView, VBuffer<T>, DataViewSchema, DataViewType, the Number/Boolean/Vector types).
         Deliberately NOT the full `Microsoft.ML`: ML.NET's runtime, trainers and transforms
         (MLContext, ITransformer, the pipeline API) live in `Microsoft.ML`, and every consumer who runs a
         pipeline references it; `Microsoft.ML` in turn depends on this same `Microsoft.ML.DataView`, so
         depending on `.DataView` alone composes with WHICHEVER Microsoft.ML the consumer already has (NuGet
         unifies upward). There is no standalone `Microsoft.ML.Data` package to depend on, and `ITransformer`
         is not needed here anyway: IDataView is ML.NET's universal currency, so
         `transformer.Transform(nd.AsDataView("Features")).ToNDArray("Score")` bridges the whole pipeline with
         two conversions and no per-transformer runner.

         The range is OPEN - 2.0.0 or later - the form Microsoft.ML itself declares this same dependency in
         (Microsoft.ML 5.0.1's nuspec: Microsoft.ML.DataView version="5.0.1"). Its floor is BOTH knobs:

         (a) FLOOR = the API we compile against. Binding is never the issue: Microsoft.ML.DataView keeps
             AssemblyVersion 1.0.0.0 in every release (2.0.0, 2.0.1, 4.0.2, 4.0.3, 5.0.1 and the 6.0 previews
             checked; only the file version moves), so any resolved version binds. The floor is about members:
             2.0.0 (Nov 2022) exposes every member used here — IDataView,
             DataViewSchema.Builder, NumberDataViewType / BooleanDataViewType / VectorDataViewType,
             DataViewRowCursor / DataViewRow.GetGetter, VBuffer<T>(int, T[]) and VBufferEditor, DataViewRowId —
             all of which have existed since 1.0.0; 2.0.0 is a conservative, widely-deployed floor.
         (b) FLOOR = what a bare consumer installs. NuGet resolves LOWEST-applicable, never latest — but a
             consumer's `Microsoft.ML` (e.g. 3.0.1 / 4.0.2) carries its own `.DataView` dependency at its own
             version, so NuGet unifies upward to THAT. In practice the consumer's ML.NET version wins; this
             floor only stops an ancient one from sneaking in.

         (c) NO UPPER BOUND, on purpose. A cap below the consumer's Microsoft.ML is not a guard but a restore
             ERROR: NuGet resolves cousin ranges that do not overlap as NU1107, so the former [2.0.0, 6.0.0) would
             have failed every bridge user's restore the day they moved to Microsoft.ML 6.0.0 (the 0.80.0-beta1 QA,
             F52: a stand-in capped at [2.0.0, 4.0.0) beside Microsoft.ML 4.0.2 fails restore with NU1107; its open
             twin restores). Nor did a cap ever keep a future ML.NET from being resolved: NuGet resolves the lowest
             applicable version, and a consumer gets a newer Microsoft.ML.DataView only through their own
             Microsoft.ML. The bridge uses ML.NET's public data contract plus one private field, VBuffer<T>._values
             (the zero-copy VBuffer import), and VBufferAccessor falls back to a copy if a future ML.NET renames or
             retypes it. So a breaking ML.NET major would show at run time, not at restore - which is why CI's
             advisory "ML.NET: Test newest (net10.0, advisory)" step runs the suite on the newest Microsoft.ML,
             previews included, to see it first.

         Verified against 2.0.0 (the floor), the current releases and the newest preview by the test project's
         MLNetVersion matrix. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.DataView" Version="2.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
